-- title:
--   Joining two components of a planar map keeps it planar
-- statement:
--   For permutations $r,f$ of a finite set $A$, let $\mathrm{cyc}$ count cycles (fixed points included) and $\mathrm{comp}(r,f)$ count the classes of the equivalence relation generated by both. The pair is a planar map if
--   $$
--   \mathrm{cyc}(r)+\mathrm{cyc}(f)+\mathrm{cyc}(rf)=|A|+2\,\mathrm{comp}(r,f),
--   $$
--   i.e. equality holds in the Euler bound. If $(f,g)$ is planar and $a,b$ lie in different components of $\langle f,g\rangle$, then $\bigl((a\ b)f,\ g\bigr)$ is planar.
--
--   Gluing two planar components at a slot of each gives a planar component.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/GroupTheory/Hyperbolic/RePairing.lean#L66-L79

